@@ -0,0 +1,16 @@
+#ifndef MT6762_THERMISTOR_H
+#define MT6762_THERMISTOR_H
+#include <stdint.h>
+/* Nominal stock 10k NTC transfer function. Does not validate physical NTC,
+ * pullup calibration, current-induced offsets, ADC freshness, or charging safety.
+ * Returns 0 on success, -1 on invalid/rail/out-of-table data; keeps out unchanged. */
+int mt6357_ntc_temperature(uint32_t adc_mv, uint32_t pullup_mv,
+                          uint32_t pullup_ohms, int *out_deci_c);
+/* Exact stock sense-path arithmetic with caller-supplied measured BIF voltage
+ * and calibrated current (positive charging). com_* are the parsed numerical
+ * configuration units. no_comp suppresses only com_fg, as the original does.
+ * This pure function provides no freshness/calibration/ownership guarantee. */
+int mt6357_ntc_compensated_temperature(uint32_t adc_mv, uint32_t bif_mv,
+    uint32_t pullup_ohms, int32_t current_ua, uint32_t com_fg,
+    uint32_t com_rfg, unsigned no_comp, int *out_deci_c);
+#endif
diff --git a/tools/mt6762/hardware/storage/ADMISSION-PERFORMANCE.rst b/tools/mt6762/hardware/storage/ADMISSION-PERFORMANCE.rst
new file mode 100644
index 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000..c34fc3fd71e81c6fd7316f1d01a21d9d62335a0a
--- /dev/null
+++ b/tools/mt6762/hardware/storage/ADMISSION-PERFORMANCE.rst
@@ -0,0 +1,137 @@
+Physical storage admission cost
+===============================
+
+Capture31 (Image37/kernel52) reaches Apple launchd and spawns SpringBoard but
+does not show a home screen. Its retained framebuffer is still the console.
+The capture proves more than 17 minutes of execution; it does not measure CPU
+utilization, per-sector latency, or the time spent inside individual drivers.
+The physical MSDC reports 47,999,984 Hz. Firmware CPU clock decoding gives a
+nominal CPU0 rate near 1.5 GHz, and this payload explicitly uses one core.
+
+The two logs show a substantial startup difference, but are not a controlled
+benchmark (different kernels, real hardware versus TCG, and different device
+models). Launchd's timestamps are relative boot wall-clock observations:
+
+============================  =======================  ======================
+Event                         Physical capture31        QEMU kernel48
+============================  =======================  ======================
+SpringBoard spawned           01:01:59.787828            01:00:23.330699
+SpringBoard exec uncorked      01:08:30.600519            01:01:42.351529
+backboardd xpcproxy spawned    01:01:58.985023            01:00:21.114302
+backboardd exec uncorked       not in retained log       01:03:19.051490
+============================  =======================  ======================
+
+The emulated run has a reviewed home-screen snapshot around 1,440 seconds;
+that is the first reviewed positive snapshot, not a precise first-frame time.
+At 1,800.008 seconds its model reports 1,554,328 CMD17 sector reads, 795,816,448
+data bytes including the 512-byte EXT_CSD, and no backend errors. The laboratory
+admission path checks GIC state and skips all physical pad, clock and rail
+checks. Consequently it does not model the overhead below.
+
+Two concrete source costs
+-------------------------
+
+``bsd/dev/mt6762_msdc_bsd.c:disk_strategy`` advertises/accepts up to 128 KiB,
+then calls the synchronous, mutex-serialized transport separately for every
+512-byte sector. There is no media cache or read-ahead inside this adapter;
+this does not establish the state of APFS/VFS caches above it. The protocol
+uses CMD13, CMD17, CMD13, draining the FIFO in eight 64-byte chunks. It does
+not use multi-block commands or DMA.
+
+``mt6762_msdc_protocol.h`` invokes ``quiet`` seven times per successful sector:
+twice for each CMD13 and three times for the data transfer. Previously every
+``quiet`` called the full physical ``admit`` implementation. On its successful
+path, each admission performed 86 direct clock/pad/tuning/GIC MMIO reads and
+ten synchronous PWRAP/PMIC reads. Therefore one sector required 70 PMIC reads;
+a 128 KiB BSD request required 17,920. This excludes the wrapper's own status
+reads and command/clear writes. Each MMIO read in the MSDC consumer includes
+``dsb oshld``. These counts follow directly from the code, not measured timing.
+
+Also, ``GenericARMCPU::start`` zero-initializes ``ml_processor_info_t`` without
+setting ``powergate_latency``. ``ml_processor_register`` converts this zero to
+``cpu_idle_latency``; ``ml_delay_should_spin`` returns false for zero. Thus
+``delay_for_interval(1, NSEC_PER_USEC)`` in MSDC and ``delay(10)`` in PWRAP can
+call ``assert_wait_deadline``/``thread_block`` whenever interrupts/preemption
+are enabled. A requested microsecond delay can yield to other work while the
+transport mutex remains held. This is a source-derived scheduling path; the
+capture has no measurements of the number or duration of such deschedulings.
+
+The MSDC wrapper now waits for its short interval using ``mach_absolute_time``
+and ARM ``YIELD``. Interrupts and preemption remain enabled. It deliberately
+does not call ``machine_delay_until``, whose ARM implementation uses ``WFE``
+and can wait for an event. The protocol's error/deadline/iteration checks are
+unchanged. The parent task separately added ``pwrap_wacs_delay`` in
+``bsd/dev/mt6762_power.c``: WACS pauses of at most 10 microseconds use the same
+timer/YIELD approach, while longer pauses and the separate ADC/thermal waits
+retain their prior delay semantics. WACS register validation and transaction
+timeouts are unchanged. ``hardware/power/test_wacs_delay.py`` compiles that
+actual wrapper against a 13 MHz mock timer, including zero, wrap and long-delay
+fallback cases.
+
+Bounded static observations
+---------------------------
+
+``mt6762_msdc_admission.h`` moves only pad, preserved tuning and twice-read
+PMIC status observations into a bounded reuse window. It does not grant
+ownership. The exact original platform, clock and GIC checks still surround
+every admission; all controller/DMA/CQE/card checks remain in the unchanged
+protocol. Only the existing exclusive synchronous inherited-LK producer
+contract enables this physical path.
+
+An observation may be reused only while less than 1,000 microseconds have
+elapsed since the BEGINNING of its full admission, and for at most 256
+completed sectors. Both bounds apply; neither is an electrical guarantee.
+The beginning timestamp makes a slow full check consume its own reuse budget.
+A full check taking at least 1 ms still admits the immediately observed state
+but grants no reusable window. Time reversal, any admission error, any sector
+protocol error, and initialization/publication boundaries invalidate reuse.
+An expiry encountered during an admission causes a new full observation at the
+next admission. Scheduling during the first cheap checks is accounted for
+before deciding whether to reuse. Scheduling during the last cheap checks
+invalidates an expired window before it can be reused again.
+
+The single transport owner is serialized by ``msdc_bsd_lock``. The window may
+span consecutive calls, including calls from different threads under that
+same lock; it is not an assertion that a complete 128 KiB request is atomic.
+The PMIC fields are digital configuration/enable readbacks, not measured rail
+voltages. An autonomous secure-firmware pad or rail change can remain unseen
+until the next full pass. No observed snapshot, including the previous
+twice-read policy, proves continuous voltage or secure-world ownership.
+The explicit 1 ms/128 KiB policy is an implementation bound, not a recovered
+hardware timing requirement. No PLL, voltage, tuning, bus-mode or DMA writes
+are introduced by this change.
+
+Counters and validation
+-----------------------
+
+``MT6762_MSDC_ADMISSION`` logs the policy. ``MT6762_MSDC_PERF`` reports after
+the first completed sector, no more than once per 30 seconds of subsequent
+read activity, and on an error. Counters are cumulative:
+
+* ``sectors``/``bytes`` count successful media reads, excluding EXT_CSD.
+* ``wall_us`` is elapsed wall time since the first media read.
+* ``read_us``/``read_max_us`` include all time inside the serialized sector
+  operation, including descheduling; they exclude waiting to acquire the BSD
+  lock and the diagnostic print after the operation.
+* ``admissions``/``full``/``reused`` count physical admission paths; they include
+  initialization. The lab bypass does not increment these physical counters.
+* ``full_us``/``full_max_us`` time full admissions including dynamic checks;
+  ``slow_full`` counts those at least 1 ms, which cannot grant reuse.
+* ``admission_errors`` and ``clock_reverse`` identify rejected observation
+  paths. Protocol errors also invalidate reuse and use the existing terminal
+  error log.
+
+These metrics distinguish a useful observation window from one defeated by
+expensive full checks. No speedup on the phone is claimed before these fields
+are captured. A synthetic protocol-integrated test confirms seven dynamic
+admissions, three commands and unchanged data while a fast modeled sector
+needs ten PMIC observations instead of seventy. Tests cover all seven failure
+positions before/after a cached observation, unchanged failed output, terminal
+MMIO silence, exact expiry, slow observation, scheduling gaps, timer reversal
+and the sector cap with a stopped mock timer. A separate test compiles the
+actual native poll-delay function, replacing only ARM YIELD and the timer
+functions, and checks zero delay, normal expiry, descheduling and counter wrap.
+
+Run ``sh tools/mt6762/hardware/storage/test.sh``. Artifact and source hashes are
+in ``admission-performance-evidence.json``. Kernel headers, builders, physical
+catalogs and phone partitions are outside this change.
diff --git a/tools/mt6762/hardware/storage/BULK-PIO.rst b/tools/mt6762/hardware/storage/BULK-PIO.rst
new file mode 100644
index 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000..830af29e5297637cc23a1a691a75d3c8151c5341
--- /dev/null
+++ b/tools/mt6762/hardware/storage/BULK-PIO.rst
@@ -0,0 +1,120 @@
+Bounded predefined multi-block PIO
+==================================
+
+The inherited controller remains in its verified PIO/card/clock/rail/tuning
+configuration. This change adds no DMA, clock changes, reset, media writes,
+partition switches or interrupt ownership. It addresses an observed throughput
+cost: APFS grouped up to128KiB, but the BSD adapter previously decomposed that
+into256 independently locked512-byte reads, each CMD13/CMD17/CMD13.
+
+Source and exact-artifact evidence
+---------------------------------
+
+The dumped Image SHA256 is
+82b1919185a49f268a0f8e145a6714d6264c1f66918a90df0d763266147ead4b.
+Its msdc_command_start range0xffffff8008afb07c..0xffffff8008afb704 hashes to
+bc7da45228f50ccba7864dbc7914e2f17cf1d36f1f3bd83ec826f3d025bee1c4,
+matching the prior symbolized ELF audit. The raw Image mapping base is
+0xffffff8008080000. Its command jump table at0xffffff80090fa0c8 selects:
+
+* CMD17:0xffffff8008afb1a0, dtype1 (0x800).
+* CMD18:0xffffff8008afb500, dtype2 (0x1000) for reads; optional auto-command
+  bits are separate branches and are not selected by this implementation.
+* CMD23:0xffffff8008afb2c8, no data-transfer dtype.
+
+The selected BSP LK platform/common/storage/mmc/msdc.c:749..774 constructs
+opcode|response<<7|blocklength<<16 and dtype2 for multi-block reads. Its
+msdc_pio_bread at1697..1742 demonstrates a variable block count and PIO length.
+The explicit mmc_set_blk_count helper in mmc_core.c:2017 is CTP-gated: it is
+reference code, not proof that the dumped LK executed that branch. The selected
+kernel-4.14/drivers/mmc/core/block.c:2395..2423 describes predefined transfers
+and issues CMD23 with block count;3987..3992 enables it for MMC versions3+.
+The existing native admission requires EXT_CSD, compatible HS timing and modern
+card capabilities. Actual success on this card remains a physical test boundary.
+
+Protocol and publication
+------------------------
+
+A single sector keeps the previous CMD13/CMD17/CMD13 path. A batch of2..256
+sectors uses exactly CMD13(0x8d), CMD23(0x97,count),
+CMD18(0x02001092,start LBA), CMD13. CMD23's upper reliable-write/data-tag/packed
+flags remain zero. SDC_BLK_NUM is programmed to the same bounded count and its
+readback checked. There is no autoCMD12/23 and no open-ended read command.
+
+The card must remain READY_FOR_DATA/TRAN after CMD23, and return READY/TRAN
+with empty FIFO, no pending interrupts, quiescent DMA/CQE and unchanged owned
+controller/clock state after completion. Exactly count*512 bytes and hardware
+data-completion status are required. Errors win over simultaneous completion.
+Progress deadlines and finite polling counts remain active, including frozen
+and backward clocks; progress never turns a hardware CRC into success.
+
+Any ambiguous partial operation latches the existing terminal fault. In normal
+Linux an erroneous predefined transfer may require CMD12 recovery. This narrow
+consumer deliberately does not attempt that recovery, reset or retry; it stops
+using the controller. An unsupported CMD23 does not silently fall back.
+
+The BSD adapter holds one transport mutex across a strategy request. A single
+128KiB heap bounce is allocated before publication, rather than on the kernel
+stack or per request. Output is copied only after all transfer and post-state
+checks pass. Failed strategy requests retain their full residual and unchanged
+output. Near-EOF success publishes only the valid prefix and leaves the exact
+remaining residual; negative, unaligned and out-of-slice requests are rejected.
+Unaligned character reads retain their one-sector scratch and release the mutex
+before uiomove, whose faults can recursively need filesystem reads.
+
+At128KiB this changes768 commands to4 and1792 admission calls to8, but leaves
+32768FIFO word reads and their existing MMIO barriers. This is not DMA throughput
+and does not imply a particular physical speedup.
+
+Measurement and remaining delay
+-------------------------------
+
+``MT6762_MSDC_PERF`` retains sector/read-time/admission statistics and adds
+batches, singles, commands, cmd13/cmd17/cmd18/cmd23, command_us and data_us.
+Command counts include initial admission commands. Command timing runs from
+issuing a command to the first completion/error status; data timing runs from
+successful data-command response to completion acknowledgement (or terminal
+failure). They include descheduling and are not CPU-utilization measurements.
+Existing read_us measures inside the serialized callback, excluding time waiting
+to acquire its BSD mutex. Existing full_us measures full ownership observations.
+
+Before this change, capture36's first270s storage interval spent259.143s inside
+read_sector; capture37 spent260.312s. Final physical per-sector times average
+319.4us and319.6us respectively, about1.53MiB/s. The eight-core QEMU56 run averaged
+290.9us/sector (~1.68MiB/s), despite skipping physical pad/rail admission. Full
+physical observations consume about16% of read time; deleting those checks alone
+cannot account for most of the cost. These are different runs, not controlled
+before/after benchmarks. Storage-relative wall intervals must not be equated
+with launchd's timestamp origin.
+
+Capture37's eight physical cores reached window-server-ready at279.715s,
+compared with278.206s in two-core capture36. Capture37 still had no donor-unlock
+marker by its retained end (~13minutes), while QEMU56's600s final frame showed
+a home screen. Cold PIO cost is concrete, but a later Apple service/main-thread
+delay remains a separate possibility. ``kern.apfs_stats`` already reports
+metadata cache hits/misses, read/pagein bytes, decompression and per-process
+lock waits; a bounded read-only sampler can distinguish these without guessing
+cache amplification. No such counter sample is asserted by the old captures.
+
+Validation
+----------
+
+``sh tools/mt6762/hardware/storage/test.sh`` passes existing single-sector,
+clock, admission and poll-delay cases plus:
+
+* Actual bulk protocol at2/32/256sectors; exact command sequence, payload/canaries,
+  count/capacity/overlap rejection and unchanged failure output.
+* Command/data/ownership/ack/post-state faults, simultaneous error+completion,
+  missing data/completion, normal/frozen/backward clocks and terminal no-retry.
+* Extracted actual BSD request/lock code: one callback per128KiB, correct slice
+  offset, partial EOF residual, full failed residual, no writes and no transport
+  calls for invalid requests.
+* Extracted actual BSD publication and cleanup: all six registration failure
+  points remove exactly the previously created nodes/registrations, clear the
+  callback and capacities, leave ready false, and reject subsequent attempts.
+  Successful whole-disk/slice publication and malformed extent admission are
+  also exercised. This caught coverage missing from the first strategy-only
+  harness; review found and corrected the stale sector_read cleanup name.
+
+These are host protocol/control-flow tests. Genuine kernel build, updated QEMU
+model and physical correctness/performance are separate acceptance steps.
diff --git a/tools/mt6762/hardware/storage/DATA-TIMEOUT.rst b/tools/mt6762/hardware/storage/DATA-TIMEOUT.rst
