index 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000..e2c273eb497c4d04a6e62e90d376cf37431dd4c7
--- /dev/null
+++ b/tools/mt6762/physical/RESET-ROUTING.rst
@@ -0,0 +1,121 @@
+Pinned preloader and DA reset routing
+====================================
+
+Follow-up: ``DA-SHUTDOWN-WIRE.rst`` resolves the receive-length gap below.
+The exact receiver rejects the 32-byte stock request before copying its
+fields, although the handler still returns a zero status. The original
+field-dispatch replay remains conditional on accepted input.
+
+The captured RGU reset acknowledgements do not establish recovery or XNU entry.
+The observed transient USB identity0e8d:0003 must not be treated as proof of
+misrouting: the user confirms this BROM enumeration can precede normal boot.
+BCB consumption is a later LK decision. This audit explains specific software
+paths without changing the phone or proposing an arbitrary flag modification.
+
+Artifacts and reproducibility
+-----------------------------
+
+``audit_reset_routing.py`` reads the exact retained preloader and original
+MTK_DA_V5 loader. It checks full SHA-256 identities before extracting entry22's
+DA2 at file0x7538a4, length0x5118c, runtime0x40000000. The original DA2 hash is
+658ff869c301fadc1a84a9409a55b5b5bdcf01524a1f904aad29a89a07ef2dec.
+The complete loader/preloader hashes and bounded code ranges are retained in
+``reset-routing-evidence.json``. No target bytes are distributed.
+
+With the existing Python capstone/unicorn dependencies::
+
+  python3 audit_reset_routing.py --preloader /path/to/preloader.bin \
+    --loader /path/to/MTK_DA_V5.bin --output /private/reset-routing.json
+
+Four original preloader safe-mode executions, four original DA dispatcher
+executions and two original DA watchdog-reset executions pass. The dispatcher
+uses controlled transport, connected-USB status, USB-disconnect, RTC-reset-time
+and delay callbacks. Its board capability routines execute their original
+instructions. The watchdog helper runs its original floating-point conversion
+and MMIO sequence against plain modeled memory, stopping at SWRST. No physical
+register, storage or USB operation is performed.
+
+Observed flag is not an enabled download request
+------------------------------------------------
+
+The pre-reset USBDL_FLAG value0x444cfffc at0x1001a080 has enablebit0 clear.
+In the supplied mt6765 register definitions, bit1 selects the USB-download
+handler and bits15:2 encode its timeout. The timeout field is all ones, but
+the download-enable bit is still zero. Do not equate magic+maximum timeout
+with an enabled forced-BROM request.
+
+The exact preloader implementation at0x223aa4 corroborates the encoding. It
+uses literal USBDL_FLAG0x1001a080 and magic0x444c0000, converts nonzero timeout
+from milliseconds, selects0xfffc for timeout0, and ORs bit0 only when en!=0.
+It writes the MISC_LOCK key0xad98, ORs RST_CON bit0, releases the lock and writes
+the resulting flag. Its exact output for en0/timeout0 is0x444cfffc; en1/timeout0
+is0x444cfffd. The captured value therefore matches disabled safe mode.
+
+The exact image has safe-mode disable calls with arguments0,0 at0x21c3f6 and
+0x21c45e, and an early enable call with arguments1,5000 at0x21c4fe. These are
+software-path facts, not evidence that one of those calls ran in the latest
+trial. The generic BSP's optional USB-auto-detect reset flow must not be assumed
+active merely from its source: its distinctive strings are absent from the
+retained image, and an aligned direct-call scan found only these calls and the
+emergency wrapper at0x223b00. This bounded search does not prove every possible
+indirect path absent.
+
+NORMAL and HOME_SCREEN are different DA command paths
+----------------------------------------------------
+
+The local mtkclient ``xflash_lib.py`` sends SHUTDOWN0x010007 followed by eight
+little-endian words::
+
+  hasflags, enablewdt, async_mode, bootmode, dl_bit, dont_resetrtc, leaveusb, 0
+
+Its NORMAL/default call sends eight zeros. HOME_SCREEN (bootmode1) sets hasflags
+and sends1,0,0,1,0,0,0,0. The routine hardcodes enablewdt0, dont_resetrtc0 and
+leaveusb0. These names and comments alone do not establish the DA's behavior.
+
+The pinned ``cmd_shutdown`` begins at0x400054ac. Its receive callback is supplied
+an initial28-byte length for the seven consumed fields; the audit copies that
+bounded prefix of the eight-word host request. Actual transport length handling
+is outside this controlled replay. With connected USB modeled, the code invokes
+its USB-disconnect and RTC-reset-time callbacks before testing hasflags.
+
+* hasflags0 branches at0x40005544 to0x400055fa, then enters the loop at0x400055fc.
+  It does not invoke the watchdog-reset callback on this path. The external
+  USB/RTC callbacks may have effects not represented by this audit; this is not
+  a claim that every physical shutdown operation is inert.
+* hasflags1/bootmode1 sets bit0 in the callback flags and invokes table
+  0x4004f5a0+0x18 at0x400055ee..55f8. This pointer is0x40017451, a Thumb thunk
+  to0x400170a0. r0 is the low16 bits of the received enablewdt word; r1 is1.
+  There is no conditional suppressing this call when enablewdt is zero.
+* Both controlled HOME_SCREEN cases, enablewdt0 and enablewdt1, reach the same
+  callback with first argument0 or1 respectively. The original capability
+  functions at0x4003a3ec and0x4003a3f0 return zero, so this image does not take
+  the alternate cold-reset/fastboot-capability branches. A bootmode2 input
+  reaches the same callback with flags0x10; that alone does not prove a usable
+  physical fastboot reboot.
+
+DA reset preparation differs from LK reset preparation
+-----------------------------------------------------
+
+The original DA helper at0x400170a0, invoked with flags1 and first argument0 or1,
+produces the following modeled sequence from STATUS0, MODE0x64 and the observed
+USBDL_FLAG0x444cfffc:
+
+1. Write0x95000000 to LATCH_CTL2 at0x10007048.
+2. Write0x22000004 to MODE at0x10007000.
+3. Clear USBDL_FLAG at0x1001a080 to zero.
+4. Invoke the delay callback with argument0 for these two tested inputs.
+5. Write SWRST0x1209 at0x10007014.
+
+The original LK reset helper preserves different MODE bits, preserves the
+USBDL flag, and disables PWRAP before SWRST (see ``lk-reset-review.rst``).
+These are materially different software sequences. Replaying the LK sequence
+from DA therefore does not reproduce every DA-reset prerequisite. Conversely,
+this difference does not establish that the remaining flag caused BROM entry,
+or authorize replacing it with a guessed value: the observed flag already has
+its enable bit clear, and the boot ROM's interpretation and reset-retention
+semantics are not available in the audited artifacts.
+
+No phone state was changed by this investigation. The current physical outcome
+still requires a later-stage observation such as a verified loader trace,
+LK/serial evidence or visible payload output. An ACK, a short-lived USB identity
+or the BCB's contents alone cannot establish that result.
diff --git a/tools/mt6762/physical/SSPM-CAPTURE33.rst b/tools/mt6762/physical/SSPM-CAPTURE33.rst
new file mode 100644
index 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000..b30acd6625abd5fc6556ea2bc5fa4c853091600e
--- /dev/null
+++ b/tools/mt6762/physical/SSPM-CAPTURE33.rst
@@ -0,0 +1,104 @@
+Capture33 SSPM prerequisite interpretation
+========================================
+
+The new clock and SRAM-control observations match the previously recorded
+screening values. The five SECURITY_AO zeros do NOT establish that CSRAM is
+unrestricted. Keep ``sspm-inherited-probe`` and DVFS ownership disabled; do not
+replace the expected security profile with a zero-accepting predicate.
+
+Capture identity and actual loads
+--------------------------------
+
+Capture33 is linked to Image38/kernel53 by trace identity
+``ab1f5e0df19932df6f498b03af28f543e7029705f50e04dd4ea2309e173a71c1``.
+The peripheral record is complete (state2, pass2, last index243). All nine
+new entries have valid3 and matching first/second values. The trace reports
+entry EL1. Raw record hash, decoded JSON hash and code/artifact hashes are
+recorded separately in ``sspm-capture33-evidence.json``.
+
+The exact Image38 loader instruction at0x40086a44 is ``ldr w4,[x4]``. It stores
+the result at0x40086a50 and then ORs the pass-valid bit at0x40086a5c..0x40086a60.
+The address and width come from the record entry at0x40086a94..0x40086aa0.
+Group0 is admitted directly by0x40086a84..0x40086a90. Therefore these zeros
+are completed load results, not untouched initialized slots or skipped
+prerequisites. No exception was retained for these accesses. That establishes
+completion of the access, not visibility of the underlying security state.
+
+================ ============ ==============================================
+Address          Both passes  Interpretation
+================ ============ ==============================================
+0x10000040       0x00020101   AXI source1, gate7 clear
+0x10000090       0x01010201   SSPM source1, gate7 clear
+0x10006350       0xffff0000   Matches BSP all-bank SRAM screening candidate
+0x10006358       0x00000012   Matches BSP SSPM SRAM screening candidate
+0x1001a010       0x00000000   Does not match PL region0/domain0 permission1
+0x1001a024       0x00000000   Domain0 field0 alone matches; full word differs
+0x1001a050       0x00000000   Does not match PL split/enables0x9000dc00
+0x1001a054       0x00000000   Does not match PL split0xdc00
+0x1001a058       0x00000000   Does not match PL split0xdc00
+================ ============ ==============================================
+
+INFRA0x100010c8 remains zero, including MCU_PM_BCLK gate17. MAINPLL's three
+captured words remain0xd4800181,0x81150000,0x80000001. Those facts strengthen
+the external clock/power screening but do not prove SRAM permissions, SSPM
+firmware execution, or mailbox ownership. SRAM field meanings and bank mapping
+are still BSP-derived rather than independently characterized hardware.
+
+What zero would mean, and what it does not prove
+----------------------------------------------
+
+The matching MT6765 BSP ``tz_sec_cfg.c:tz_sram_enable_region_protection`` calls
+bit28..31 clear "disable Region n protection". ``tz_sec_reg.h`` defines
+permission0 as secure/nonsecure read/write. Accordingly, IF the five returned
+words expose the actual enabled-region and permission state, they describe
+disabled region protection with permission fields0. This is a conditional
+register interpretation, not proof that the condition holds for EL1 reads.
+
+The exact dumped preloader uses the same addresses and fields. Its literal
+at0x2281e4 is0x1001a010; the caller passes0x10dc00 at0x2280ea..0x2280f2.
+Instructions0x22815c..0x228176 set all three split words to0xdc00 and enable
+regions0/3. Instructions0x228178..0x228198 set low CTRL fields to0xb69;
+0x22819a..0x2281ba set the relevant CTRL5 fields to0x01680000. There is no
+conditional zero result in that initializer when its ordinary loads/stores
+take effect. Thus all-zero EL1 results do not match its configured profile.
+
+There is also historical physical corroboration, not just source inference:
+``hardware/mtk-unlock-20261024/expdb-posttrial-complete.bin`` at byte0x698f88
+contains the preloader's AFTER log showing split/enables0x9000dc00, splits1/2
+0xdc00, CTRL0xb69 and CTRL5 0x1680000. The preceding BEFORE log shows zero.
+Other retained boots include CTRL bit31 before/after; its meaning is not
+assumed here. These historical logs are not a secure-world observation from
+the same boot as capture33, and cannot establish current state.
+
+The exact ATF DEVAPC initializer0x47d94814..0x47d94910 and permission table
+at0x47d9eaf0 still corroborate domain0 permission0 for SECURITY_AO entry26 and
+SRAMROM entry52. DEVAPC AO itself is secured at0x47d94834. An outer bus-domain
+permission does not prove that individual security registers expose their
+secure values to nonsecure reads. No exact hardware description or recovered
+ATF operation establishes whether these five words read as zero in the
+nonsecure view. Nor has a later reset/clear of the SRAM protection registers
+been demonstrated. Neither explanation is established by this capture.
+
+Admission consequence and next discriminating evidence
+------------------------------------------------------
+
+Classify the five words as ``completed_nonsecure_zero_view_unknown``. Their
+stability is useful evidence but is not an access grant. In particular,
+CTRL5's zero domain0 field must not be accepted independently of the unknown
+region-enable/split and register-visibility state.
+
+The missing discriminator is a trustworthy view of these same five words
+after the current secure initialization, paired with the EL1 view and bound
+to the exact firmware boot. This can be obtained only through an already
+audited read-only secure-firmware query or a separately reviewed same-boot
+secure-stage observation; neither mechanism is established here. A nonzero
+BOOT_MISC register elsewhere in SECURITY_AO would demonstrate that the block
+responds, but would not settle access semantics for the five security words.
+Do not use a speculative SMC, permission write, direct secure-only DEVAPC read,
+or an unadmitted CSRAM/mailbox access as a substitute.
+
+Mailbox DEVAPC permissions and CSRAM region permissions are separate gates;
+these SRAM zeros neither prove nor disprove mailbox permission. The current
+observer combines both regions, so it remains disabled until its whole
+powered/readable contract is established. This analysis adds evidence and
+documentation only; no grants, kernel/header changes or phone operations.
diff --git a/tools/mt6762/physical/SSPM-GUARDED-PROBE-DESIGN.rst b/tools/mt6762/physical/SSPM-GUARDED-PROBE-DESIGN.rst
